WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.834
[ Cheering and applause ]
That's --

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:04.300
You know what?
-I'm so confused.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.601
-That's a lot of love.
-Yeah, that's so sweet.

00:00:06.601 --> 00:00:08.033
I'm like genuinely taken aback.

00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:09.834
-Yeah, good.
-I don't know what to do now.

00:00:09.834 --> 00:00:11.968
-I feel that way, too, though.
-Okay.

00:00:11.968 --> 00:00:13.133
-You've only been
on our show once,

00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:14.767
but I feel like I want to be
friends with you.

00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:15.801
I love you.
-Should we?

00:00:15.801 --> 00:00:17.133
-Yeah.
-Done.

00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:18.133
-You want to?
-Yeah.

00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:19.801
Cool.

00:00:19.801 --> 00:00:21.868
[ Laughter, cheering, applause ]

00:00:21.868 --> 00:00:22.701
It's a high--

00:00:24.601 --> 00:00:26.434
You totally did it.
I know, I know.

00:00:26.434 --> 00:00:29.300
How is "Saturday Night Live"
going?

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.501
-It's going really well,
actually.

00:00:30.501 --> 00:00:32.601
Everyone is really cool.
It's very comfortable.

00:00:32.601 --> 00:00:34.000
I feel like I'm still --

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.300
I forget that they've been
doing it for so long.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.100
So, like things happen
[ Snaps fingers ] so quickly.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.367
-Oh, yeah.
-We were deciding
the sketches the other day,

00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:42.934
and I remember
I was sitting in the chair

00:00:42.934 --> 00:00:44.167
across from Lorne Michaels,

00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:46.167
and everyone started
getting up and leaving.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.133
And I looked at Lorne, and I was
like, "Oh, are we done?"

00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:50.501
And he was like, "Yeah."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:52.334
-"Yeah, we moved on."
-I was sitting there

00:00:52.334 --> 00:00:53.667
for maybe 30 seconds.
Like it did not feel --

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:55.234
-There's no more scripts
to read. That's it.

00:00:55.234 --> 00:00:56.434
-We go through things
so quickly.

00:00:56.434 --> 00:00:57.801
-Is it because you're savoring
every moment?

00:00:57.801 --> 00:00:59.133
-Maybe, I guess you could say.

00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:00.334
But also, at the same time,

00:01:00.334 --> 00:01:02.200
I'm so in awe
of everybody around me.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.167
I'm also,
I guess, being really quiet.

00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:07.534
Because I was looking and,
as we were taking the sketches,

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:08.634
he was like,
"Is there a sketch you prefer?

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:09.767
This one or this one?"

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:11.000
I said, "Oh, I think
that one's funnier,"

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.734
and he said, "Okay.
Well, then say something."

00:01:12.734 --> 00:01:14.067
And I was like, "Oh!"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:15.501
-That's right. Exactly right.
-"Yeah, you're right."

00:01:15.501 --> 00:01:17.200
-Obviously, you learned
some lessons

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.667
out of "Saturday Night Live."
-I think I'm learning.

00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:19.968
I'm picking up on it now.
-Yeah, it's the best crew

00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:22.200
up there, the best writers,
the best hair and makeup.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.367
It's so much fun.
-Yeah, they're so seasoned.

00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:26.467
-Yeah, it's great.
You're gonna be fantastic.

00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:27.767
Break a leg this Saturday.
Come on.

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:30.767
We'll be watching.
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:34.267
We'll be watching
and supporting.

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:36.200
Last time we were here, we were
talking about "Wednesday."

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.634
And, man, oh, man, it turned out
one of the biggest shows

00:01:38.634 --> 00:01:40.934
in the world on Netflix.
Congratulations.

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.868
-Thank you.
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:01:43.868 --> 00:01:46.834
-And since you've been here,
it got picked up for Season 2,

00:01:46.834 --> 00:01:48.868
so there you go.
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:50.434
More work.

00:01:50.434 --> 00:01:51.701
Work breeds work.

00:01:51.701 --> 00:01:53.133
Now you got to work more.
-Yeah.

00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:56.033
No, I'm very excited.
-Do you have any idea

00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:57.801
where you're gonna go
this season at all?

00:01:57.801 --> 00:01:59.701
-I don't know.
I think we just started

00:01:59.701 --> 00:02:01.934
getting a writers' room together
and kind of talking about it.

00:02:01.934 --> 00:02:03.400
And everyone's
being really cool.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.934
I think we want to up kind of
the horror aspect a little bit

00:02:05.934 --> 00:02:09.267
and then get Wednesday
out of the romantic situation

00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:11.400
and just let her be
her own individual

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.968
and fight her own crime...
-Yeah!

00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:14.567
-...and battles [indistinct].

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:17.133
[ Cheering, whistling,
and applause ]

00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:19.934
-You've been nominated
for every single award.

00:02:19.934 --> 00:02:21.501
Congratulations.
Well-deserved. I, honestly...

00:02:21.501 --> 00:02:23.400
-Thank you.
-...I really can't get enough.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.801
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:02:24.801 --> 00:02:27.834
Do you like going to these award
shows and doing the whole thing?

00:02:27.834 --> 00:02:29.901
-I don't think --
going to award shows,

00:02:29.901 --> 00:02:32.267
the 30 minutes before,
it's the most sick

00:02:32.267 --> 00:02:33.767
you'll ever feel
in your entire life.

00:02:33.767 --> 00:02:38.334
You're so anxious, so nervous,
so whatever, and especially --

00:02:38.334 --> 00:02:39.801
You're with people
that you admire.

00:02:39.801 --> 00:02:42.133
Like the SAG Awards, it was,
you know, all of your peers,

00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:43.400
everyone who's a part of SAG,

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.567
so you're seeing
these people like --

00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:45.934
-You had a hot table.
I loved your table.

00:02:45.934 --> 00:02:47.234
-Yeah, I had
a really cool table.

00:02:47.234 --> 00:02:50.200
-Yeah, Zendaya was there.
-Yeah, well, Zendaya,

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.868
I feel like we've kind of --
we've met and not met

00:02:52.868 --> 00:02:55.267
so many times, I feel like,
this time, we were actually

00:02:55.267 --> 00:02:57.133
able to have
a nice conversation.

00:02:57.133 --> 00:02:58.501
But I was kind of freaking out
because she was sitting next

00:02:58.501 --> 00:03:01.667
to Paul Mescal, who I think is
so talented and so good.

00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:02.868
And I was so nervous
to meet him,

00:03:02.868 --> 00:03:04.400
and I knew he was gonna be
at my table.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.634
And I remember him --
he introduced himself to me

00:03:06.634 --> 00:03:08.367
when I wasn't expecting it.
-Ooh.

00:03:08.367 --> 00:03:09.834
-So he was like,
"Oh, hi! Nice to meet you.

00:03:09.834 --> 00:03:11.334
I'm Paul."
And I was so nervous,

00:03:11.334 --> 00:03:12.567
I kind of couldn't look him
in the eye,

00:03:12.567 --> 00:03:14.033
so I fear that
it was a bit like --

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:17.267
-You were like, "Yeah,
yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah."

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:19.701
-"Yeah, 'Aftersun' was great.
-Oh, my God.

00:03:19.701 --> 00:03:21.067
-You did that."
-I've done that before,

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.501
and you go, "Oh, my God.
That's so rude."

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:24.167
But you're just nervous,

00:03:24.167 --> 00:03:25.634
and "You get me
at the wrong moment."

00:03:25.634 --> 00:03:28.434
-I have that happen so many
times to me at award shows.

00:03:28.434 --> 00:03:30.067
-But, no, the best thing,
I think,

00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:32.701
was you and Aubrey Plaza
at the SAG Awards.

00:03:32.701 --> 00:03:36.067
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:03:36.067 --> 00:03:39.534
It was so -- it was so funny,
and it destroyed.

00:03:39.534 --> 00:03:41.067
It was a great bit.

00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:43.033
And then the Internet went
on fire.

00:03:43.033 --> 00:03:44.601
And it was like, "We want
to see you guys in a movie.

00:03:44.601 --> 00:03:47.100
Do anything together.
Do something. Play sisters."

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.601
-Yeah, I would do anything
with her.

00:03:48.601 --> 00:03:50.000
She's another one of those
people where everyone

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.934
kept saying, "Oh, you remind me
of Aubrey,"

00:03:51.934 --> 00:03:53.434
or, you know, "Oh, you two
should get together,"

00:03:53.434 --> 00:03:55.667
and we just never did
until we were backstage then.

00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:57.200
And she just looked at me,
she said, "Hey, you!

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:58.200
[ Snaps fingers ]
Get over here."

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.467
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah!

00:03:59.467 --> 00:04:01.033
See? She's so funny.
-Yeah, she was really cool.

00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:02.801
-She's smart.
She's everything, yeah.

00:04:02.801 --> 00:04:04.434
Can we talk about "Scream VI"?
-Yeah.

00:04:04.434 --> 00:04:06.400
-It's a big deal.
Tomorrow.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.400
[ Cheering and applause ]
It's out tomorrow.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.667
"Scream VI" is set in New York.

00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:11.734
Oh, yeah.
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:04:11.734 --> 00:04:14.634
New York City,
where we do our show.

00:04:14.634 --> 00:04:17.033
How do we set up this "Scream"?

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:19.767
-Well, this time,
it's kind of --

00:04:19.767 --> 00:04:21.267
Well, yeah, it's a different
setting, which I mean,

00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:23.601
the "Scream" franchise
has never left Woodsboro,

00:04:23.601 --> 00:04:25.400
so I think that it makes
for a lot more

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.367
an intimidating story,
I would say.

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:30.934
You know, Ghostface can come
for you on the subway,

00:04:30.934 --> 00:04:33.200
push you off a skyscraper
building.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:34.501
You know.
-Yeah.

00:04:34.501 --> 00:04:35.901
-New York City
has got a lot of places.

00:04:35.901 --> 00:04:39.300
-Everything about this --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.133
I think everything about this
movie is just bigger

00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:44.000
and scarier, and also Ghostface
is just so much more brutal.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.467
I feel like
he's very unforgiving,

00:04:45.467 --> 00:04:47.534
and everyone's a victim,
not just the lead cast,

00:04:47.534 --> 00:04:49.367
which makes it better.

00:04:49.367 --> 00:04:51.501
-Now, do you know,
when you get the script,

00:04:51.501 --> 00:04:54.300
do you know who Ghostface is,
or do they change the script

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:55.934
so that you don't really know
who it is?

00:04:55.934 --> 00:04:57.701
-Sometimes they give us
different scripts,

00:04:57.701 --> 00:04:58.801
different endings.

00:04:58.801 --> 00:05:00.434
This time around,
they just didn't give us

00:05:00.434 --> 00:05:02.801
the third act at all.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:04.734
-So you gotta see the movie
to find out who the killer is?

00:05:04.734 --> 00:05:06.400
-Yeah, now you just don't know.
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.434
-Everyone usually finds out
at least by the last two weeks.

00:05:08.434 --> 00:05:10.400
-Yeah.
-But they try to play games.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:13.133
-Congrats on that,
and congrats on "SNL."
You're gonna be fantastic.

00:05:13.133 --> 00:05:16.033
Alright. Now, Jenna, here's how
this works, okay?

00:05:16.033 --> 00:05:18.501
We have nine mystery boxes

00:05:18.501 --> 00:05:21.100
containing objects
no one has ever seen before.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.968
On your turn, you pick a box,

00:05:22.968 --> 00:05:25.334
take out the object,
describe it to your opponent.

00:05:25.334 --> 00:05:26.434
Then they have to guess

00:05:26.434 --> 00:05:28.367
if you're lying
or telling the truth.

00:05:28.367 --> 00:05:31.934
Jenna, you pick the first box.

00:05:31.934 --> 00:05:35.501
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:37.300
[ Audience shouting numbers ]

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:38.534
-Three?

00:05:38.534 --> 00:05:43.467
[ Drumroll ]

00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:44.667
-You are choosing 3.

00:05:44.667 --> 00:05:48.634
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:05:48.634 --> 00:05:51.133
Huh, it looks --
it looks light.

00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:53.200
-[ Groans ]
-Yeah, too late.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.133
Too late to act now.
-Now?

00:05:55.133 --> 00:05:57.767
-Too late to act now.
So, I know it's light.

00:05:57.767 --> 00:06:00.901
Are you going to lie or are you
going to tell the truth?

00:06:00.901 --> 00:06:03.701
Very interesting.
Jenna Ortega.

00:06:03.701 --> 00:06:06.834
[ Suspenseful music plays ]

00:06:06.834 --> 00:06:08.267
[ Tapping ]

00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:10.067
Yeah, yeah, yeah. Interesting.
Yeah, yeah.

00:06:10.067 --> 00:06:12.000
Oh, oh, I see.
You're making sounds.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.334
You're distracting me.
Yeah.

00:06:13.334 --> 00:06:15.501
-You don't know what's
happening to you.

00:06:15.501 --> 00:06:17.467
-What is in your box?

00:06:17.467 --> 00:06:18.834
-Can I be honest with you,
Jimmy?

00:06:18.834 --> 00:06:20.901
-Yes, you can always be honest
with me.

00:06:20.901 --> 00:06:22.634
-I don't -- I'm -- I'm not sure

00:06:22.634 --> 00:06:23.901
I even understand
what's happening here.

00:06:23.901 --> 00:06:25.734
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:25.734 --> 00:06:28.100
-You don't understand
what's happening?

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:31.467
So, this thing that you are now
looking at,

00:06:31.467 --> 00:06:32.767
you don't understand.
-I don't.

00:06:32.767 --> 00:06:34.133
-Can you describe it for me?

00:06:34.133 --> 00:06:35.767
-I fear of being a bit too
on the nose,

00:06:35.767 --> 00:06:39.100
but it is
"Addams Family"-themed.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:40.234
-Yeah. Well, we knew that you
were coming on.

00:06:40.234 --> 00:06:42.968
-Cousin Itt.
Yeah, well, it would make sense.

00:06:42.968 --> 00:06:44.334
-It would make sense.
We did book you.

00:06:44.334 --> 00:06:45.767
-Cousin Itt.

00:06:45.767 --> 00:06:49.701
He happens to be reading
a magazine of some sorts,

00:06:49.701 --> 00:06:52.534
perhaps like "Vanity Fair"
Chewbacca edition?

00:06:52.534 --> 00:06:54.067
[ Laughter ]

00:06:54.067 --> 00:06:55.934
-Cousin Itt is reading
a magazine

00:06:55.934 --> 00:06:59.167
and it could be "Vanity Fair."
-Yeah, on a nice bench.

00:06:59.167 --> 00:07:02.200
-A bench? So, he's outside.
-Yeah, he's outside.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:03.934
It seems to be a nice park,
Central Park,

00:07:03.934 --> 00:07:05.734
if you will, in New York.
-Oh, it's New York.

00:07:05.734 --> 00:07:08.567
So, he's in Central Park?
-There may be a corgi involved.

00:07:08.567 --> 00:07:10.200
-It's a corgi?
-Yeah.

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.067
-So, Cousin Itt owns a dog?

00:07:12.067 --> 00:07:13.501
[ Laughter ]

00:07:13.501 --> 00:07:15.334
Cousin Itt owns a pet?

00:07:15.334 --> 00:07:17.767
-I guess so.
-So there's a lot of hair.

00:07:17.767 --> 00:07:20.300
There's a lot of hair.
Is Cousin Itt wearing a hat?

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.934
-Yes, he is.
Like he always does.

00:07:22.934 --> 00:07:25.167
He always has a hat.
-Yeah.

00:07:25.167 --> 00:07:27.334
But if you took your time
answering that,

00:07:27.334 --> 00:07:29.367
that would mean
that you're lying.

00:07:29.367 --> 00:07:31.067
So, I think that you are...

00:07:32.434 --> 00:07:34.734
...telling the truth!

00:07:34.734 --> 00:07:35.934
[ Drumroll ]

00:07:35.934 --> 00:07:36.868
-I am.

00:07:36.868 --> 00:07:38.367
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:07:38.367 --> 00:07:39.467
[ Ding! ]

00:07:39.467 --> 00:07:40.934
-Wow!

00:07:40.934 --> 00:07:42.067
"Vanity Fair."
-Honestly --

00:07:42.067 --> 00:07:44.400
-Chewbacca? It's weird.
That is odd.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.300
-Yeah, I should've lied
because it's way too niche.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.767
Like the "Vanity Fair" Chewbacca
article, instantly --

00:07:49.767 --> 00:07:51.067
-Yeah, we wouldn't do that.
Alright.

00:07:51.067 --> 00:07:52.834
[ Audience shouts numbers ]

00:07:52.834 --> 00:07:54.267
[ Drumroll ]

00:07:54.267 --> 00:07:56.667
-2! 2!

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:02.334
-Oh, my back!

00:08:02.334 --> 00:08:04.934
-I already did that.
-[ Groaning ]

00:08:08.133 --> 00:08:09.534
Ooh!
-Ohh!

00:08:09.534 --> 00:08:10.701
-Ooh! Here we go.

00:08:10.701 --> 00:08:15.367
[ Suspenseful music plays ]

00:08:15.367 --> 00:08:18.968
[ Laughter ]

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:26.200
It's a painting.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:27.601
-Painting of what?

00:08:27.601 --> 00:08:29.868
-Famous, famous,
famous painting.

00:08:29.868 --> 00:08:31.400
Something you'd see in a museum.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:34.200
-I feel like you have to be
more specific on this game.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.100
-I don't think -- I don't want
to be more specific.

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:38.400
[ Laughter ]
I want to be vague.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.834
Think of a famous painting.
-Oh, so it's just a painting.

00:08:40.834 --> 00:08:43.601
Don't tell me it's
the Edvard Munch, the --

00:08:43.601 --> 00:08:45.267
-"The Scream"?!
-Ooh.

00:08:45.267 --> 00:08:47.100
-Ooh, that's interesting.
-That's a bit on the nose, too.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.000
-Well, you do have a movie
called "Scream" coming out.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.033
So, that's pretty funny,
not niche.

00:08:51.033 --> 00:08:52.400
[ Laughter ]

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:55.200
Now,

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.667
now, here's what makes it funny.

00:08:57.667 --> 00:09:02.934
It's not -- The painting's face.

00:09:02.934 --> 00:09:04.367
It's Colonel Sanders.

00:09:04.367 --> 00:09:07.634
[ Laughter ]

00:09:07.634 --> 00:09:09.167
